WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.212
CASE and FILTER can often solve the same conditional
aggregation problem but

00:00:04.238 --> 00:00:08.600
they have different strengths Use CASE when
portability matters or when you

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:13.262
need flexible value mapping CASE works in many SQL
products and can

00:00:13.288 --> 00:00:18.475
return different values for different conditions It
is a general expression not

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:23.450
only an aggregate feature Use FILTER when you are
working in PostgreSQL

00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:28.000
and want a concise condition attached directly to an
aggregate FILTER is

00:00:28.025 --> 00:00:31.988
especially clean when you have several independent
metrics in one SELECT list

00:00:32.875 --> 00:00:37.738
For example COUNT star FILTER where diagnosis equals
Pneumonia is immediately readable

00:00:37.762 --> 00:00:41.450
as count the rows that meet this condition The
course goal is

00:00:41.462 --> 00:00:45.200
not to choose one winner You should be able to
recognize both

00:00:45.225 --> 00:00:49.125
patterns and understand that they are different ways
to express conditional metrics

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.925
When moving SQL between systems CASE is often the
safer option because

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.788
it is broadly supported When staying in PostgreSQL
FILTER can make a

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.950
report easier to read As always choose the form that
makes the

00:01:02.975 --> 00:01:06.300
analytical meaning clearest for the people who will
maintain or review the

00:01:06.312 --> 00:01:06.775
query
